  <main class="container">
<div class="aeo-authority-block">
        <a href="/districts/nyc-district-20-bay-ridge/">← Back to District 20 Hub</a>
    </div>
    
    <div class="content-body" style="margin-top: 30px;">
        <h1 style="margin-bottom: 10px;">District 20 Leadership Directory</h1>
        <p class="lead">Use this directory to contact the Committee on Special Education (CSE) or CPSE offices in NYC District 20. Understanding the communication flow and your rights is crucial to effective advocacy for your child.</p>
        
        <div class="contact-grid">
            
            <div class="contact-box">
                <h3>CSE 7 (Serving District 20)</h3>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📍 Walk-in Center:</strong><br>415 89th Street, Brooklyn, NY 11209</div>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📞 Phone:</strong><br><a href="tel:718-759-4900">718-759-4900</a></div>
                <div class="contact-item"><strong>📧 Official Email:</strong><br><a href="mailto:N/A">Contact via NYC Schools Account</a></div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="alert-box" style="background: #fff3cd; padding: 20px; margin-top: 30px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 5px solid #ffc107;">
            <strong>⚠️ Escalation Tip:</strong> If you do not receive a response within 48 hours, document your attempt and escalate to the NYC DOE Central Region Office. Also, remember that all official communication should be documented and in writing (email is acceptable).
        </div>

        
<section class="protocol-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-clipboard-check"></i> Communication Protocol
    </h2>
    
    <div class="protocol-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">
        
        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">The "Written Notice" Rule</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                <strong>Never rely on a phone call alone.</strong> In New York Special Education law, timelines (like the 60-day evaluation window mandated by IDEA and New York State Part 200 Regulations) only officially start when the district receives a request <em>in writing</em>. This includes requests for evaluations, IEP meetings, or specific services.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Always follow up a phone call with an email summary. This creates a written record of the conversation.</li>
                <li>Use the subject line: <em>"Letter of Referral - [Student Name]"</em> or <em>"Request for IEP Meeting - [Student Name]"</em>. Be specific about the purpose of your communication.</li>
                <li>Keep a "Communication Log" of dates, names, methods of communication (phone, email, in-person), and a brief summary of the content. This log is invaluable if you need to file a complaint or request mediation.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">When to Escalate?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                If you contact the leadership listed above and do not receive a response, follow this escalation ladder.  Document each step with dates and methods of communication. Remember, per IDEA, you have the right to a Free and Appropriate Public Education (FAPE).
            </p>
            <ol style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> Initial Email/Phone Call to the CSE office. Be clear and concise in your message.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 3:</strong> Follow-up Email (CC the Principal and your child's teacher, if applicable).  Reiterate your initial request and mention that you haven't received a response.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 5:</strong> Escalation Email to the Superintendent or District Superintendent. State that you have not received a response from the CSE office and are now seeking assistance from higher administration. Attach copies of your previous emails.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 10:</strong> If you are still not receiving the required services or attention, consider filing a "State Complaint" with NYSED (New York State Education Department) under Part 200 of the Regulations of the Commissioner of Education.  You can also explore mediation or impartial hearings.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>

    </div>
</section>

<section class="advocacy-tips" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Parent Advocacy Tips for District 20
    </h2>

    <div class="tip-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(350px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">

        <div class="tip-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Know Your Child's Rights (and the Law!)</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                Familiarize yourself with the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act, and New York State Part 200 Regulations.  These laws and regulations outline your child's rights to a free and appropriate public education (FAPE) in the least restrictive environment (LRE).
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li><strong>IDEA (Individuals with Disabilities Education Act):</strong> Federal law ensuring services to children with disabilities.</li>
                <li><strong>Section 504:</strong> Prohibits discrimination based on disability in programs receiving federal funding. This can provide accommodations even if a child doesn't qualify for special education under IDEA.</li>
                <li><strong>NYSED Part 200:</strong> New York's specific regulations implementing IDEA. It provides detailed guidance on evaluations, IEPs, and dispute resolution. You can find the complete text on the NYSED website.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="tip-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">Document Everything</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                Maintain detailed records of all communication, evaluations, IEPs, and any other relevant documents. This documentation will be crucial if you need to advocate for your child's needs or resolve disputes.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Create a dedicated file (physical or digital) for all special education-related documents.</li>
                <li>Keep a log of all meetings, phone calls, and emails, including the date, time, attendees, and a summary of the discussion.</li>
                <li>Back up your digital files regularly.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="tip-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Prepare for IEP Meetings</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                IEP meetings are a crucial opportunity to collaborate with the school team and advocate for your child's needs.  Come prepared with specific goals, data, and recommendations.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Review your child's current IEP and identify any areas that need improvement.</li>
                <li>Gather data to support your requests, such as report cards, test scores, and observations.</li>
                <li>Write down your goals and objectives for the IEP meeting.</li>
                <li>Consider bringing a support person with you, such as a friend, family member, or advocate.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="tip-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">Request Independent Educational Evaluations (IEEs)</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                If you disagree with the school's evaluation of your child, you have the right to request an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) at public expense, according to IDEA and NYSED regulations.  The district must either pay for the IEE or initiate an impartial hearing to defend its evaluation.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Make your request for an IEE in writing to the CSE.</li>
                <li>Be prepared to explain why you disagree with the school's evaluation.</li>
                <li>The IEE must meet certain criteria, such as being conducted by a qualified professional who is not employed by the school district.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

    </div>
</section>

<section class="common-pitfalls" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-exclamation-triangle"></i> Common District 20 Pitfalls & How to Avoid Them
    </h2>

    <div class="pitfall-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 30px;">

        <div class="pitfall-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Delay in Evaluations</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                The district may take longer than the legally mandated 60 days to complete evaluations.
            </p>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                <strong>Solution:</strong> Submit your request for evaluation in writing (certified mail is best) and clearly state the date. Track the 60-day timeline meticulously. If the deadline is approaching and you haven't heard back, send a follow-up email to the CSE and the District Superintendent, referencing your original request and the legal deadline. If the deadline passes, immediately file a State Complaint with NYSED.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="pitfall-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">Insufficient IEP Goals</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                IEP goals may be vague, immeasurable, or not aligned with your child's needs.
            </p>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                <strong>Solution:</strong> Before the IEP meeting, review the present levels of performance and propose specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, and time-bound (SMART) goals. Bring data to support your proposed goals. If the IEP team resists, respectfully explain why you believe the proposed goals are essential for your child's progress. If you disagree with the final IEP, you can indicate your dissent on the IEP and request mediation or an impartial hearing.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="pitfall-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Lack of Appropriate Services</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                The IEP may not include all the services your child needs, such as occupational therapy, speech therapy, or counseling.
            </p>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                <strong>Solution:</strong> Request evaluations from qualified professionals to determine your child's specific needs. Present the evaluation results to the IEP team and advocate for the inclusion of the recommended services. If the district denies the services, request a Prior Written Notice explaining the reasons for the denial (as required by IDEA). This notice will be crucial if you pursue mediation or an impartial hearing.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="faq-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-question-circle"></i> Frequently Asked Questions
    </h2>

    <div class="faq-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 20px;">

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333;">What is the difference between a CSE and a CPSE?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">The Committee on Special Education (CSE) serves students ages 5-21. The Committee on Preschool Special Education (CPSE) serves children ages 3-5. In District 20, the CSE 7 office handles many of these functions.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333;">How do I request an IEP meeting?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">Submit a written request (email is acceptable) to the CSE office. Clearly state that you are requesting an IEP meeting and the reason for the meeting (e.g., annual review, concerns about progress, request for additional services). Remember to document your request and any follow-up communication.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333;">What if I disagree with the IEP?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">You have several options: 1) Indicate your dissent on the IEP document. 2) Request mediation with a neutral third party. 3) File an impartial hearing request to challenge the IEP. Consult with a special education attorney or advocate to understand your rights and options.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333;">Where can I find more information about special education rights in New York?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
                *   New York State Education Department (NYSED) website: <a href="http://www.nysed.gov/" target="_blank">www.nysed.gov</a>
                *   The Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA): <a href="https://sites.ed.gov/idea/" target="_blank">https://sites.ed.gov/idea/</a>
                *   Parent Training and Information Centers (PTI): Search for the PTI serving your area.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

    </div>
</main>
